  "response": "## What the scan points at\n\nI read the flagged lines to confirm the JSON's story. The scan surfaces four temporal findings; only one of them is actually reachable from `billing.ts`.\n\n| Finding | Where | Reachable from billing? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `crime_00012` Temporal Recklessness | `src/billing.ts:57,63,92,198,238,250,273` | **yes — this is the one** |\n| `crime_00009` Half-UTC, Half-Local | `src/date.ts:9,41` | no — `billing.ts` never imports `date.ts` |\n| `crime_00011` Timezone Roulette | `src/date.ts:29,33` | no |\n| `crime_00017` DST-Naive Day Math | `src/date.ts:52` | no — but see below |\n\n`date.ts` is the more spectacular file, and it's tempting to blame `mixed_utc_local_methods` (severity 0.8) for a date-boundary flake. It isn't the cause here: `grep` shows no import edge from `billing.ts` or `billing.test.ts` to `date.ts`. It's a latent hazard, not this bug.\n\n## Most likely cause\n\n**`src/billing.ts:92`** — the loyalty-discount tier — with `:273` (`recommendUpgrade`) carrying an identical copy:\n\n```ts\nconst daysSince = Math.floor((Date.now() - user.createdAt) / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24));\nif (daysSince > 365)      discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.1);\nelse if (daysSince > 180) discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.05);\nelse if (daysSince > 90)  discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.02);\n```\n\nThree defects stack into exactly the symptom described:\n\n1. **Unpinned clock** (`crime_00012`). Every run reads a different `now`, so a fixture user with a literal `createdAt` drifts across 90 / 180 / 365 as wall time passes. The suite goes green for weeks, then one morning a user crosses a threshold and the expected `discountCents` is wrong — the classic \"passed yesterday, fails today\" date-boundary flake.\n2. **Millisecond division for calendar days.** This is the same defect `crimes` charged in `date.ts:52` (`+ 86400000`), written inline here as `1000 * 60 * 60 * 24` — the DST detector didn't flag this instance. A 90-calendar-day interval spanning a DST transition is 90 days ∓ 1 hour in ms, so `Math.floor` returns **89**, and the tier silently drops. That fails only for runs whose window straddles a transition: seasonal, TZ-dependent, and it reproduces on nobody's machine.\n3. **Non-atomic time.** `Date.now()` is read independently at `:57` (`createdAt`), `:63` (`metadata.runAt`), and `:92`. Two calls milliseconds apart can land on opposite sides of midnight, so any assertion relating `invoice.createdAt` to `metadata.runAt`'s date fails on roughly the fraction of runs that hit 00:00.\n\n`Math.random()` at `:56` for the invoice id is the same non-determinism class, if any assertion touches `id`.\n\n## Caveat on confirming it\n\n`src/billing.test.ts` doesn't call `generateInvoice` — both tests invoke local no-op helpers (`crime_00025`: zero assertions; `crime_00031`: `toBeTruthy()` only). So whatever is failing isn't in the file the scan sampled, and I'm ranking by risk surface rather than pointing at a reproduction. To confirm before fixing:\n\n```bash\nTZ=America/New_York npx vitest run   # then rerun with TZ=UTC\n```\n\nA TZ-dependent diff confirms #2; a run-to-run diff under fixed TZ confirms #1/#3.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\nI haven't applied this — `01-messy-ts-app` is a detector-tuning fixture and its crimes are load-bearing.\n\n**1. Inject the clock; read it once.** Fixes #1 and #3 together:\n\n```ts\nexport function generateInvoice(\n  user: User,\n  items: InvoiceLine[],\n  paymentMethod?: string,\n  now: number = Date.now(),   // one read, threaded everywhere\n): Invoice {\n  const createdAt = now;\n  const metadata = { runAt: new Date(now).toISOString(), /* … */ };\n  // …\n  const daysSince = daysBetween(user.createdAt, now);\n}\n```\n\n**2. Make day math calendar-aware.** Count whole UTC days rather than dividing elapsed ms, so no DST transition can move a boundary:\n\n```ts\nfunction daysBetween(fromMs: number, toMs: number): number {\n  const day = (ms: number) => Math.floor(ms / 86_400_000); // UTC day index\n  return day(toMs) - day(fromMs);\n}\n```\n\nAnchoring on the UTC day index removes the offset entirely. If tiers are meant to be evaluated in the customer's zone, use `Temporal.PlainDate.from(...).since(...)` with an explicit `timeZone` instead — but pick one and write it down, because `:92` and `:273` currently disagree with nothing and each other by accident.\n\n**3. Deduplicate.** `:273` holds a second copy of the tier logic (also flagged by `magic_domain_literal_scatter`, `crime_00004`). Extract one `loyaltyTier(daysSince)` so the boundary is fixed in one place.\n\n**4. Pin time in the tests and assert the boundary.** Right now nothing protects this:\n\n```ts\nbeforeEach(() => { vi.useFakeTimers(); vi.setSystemTime(new Date(\"2026-03-15T12:00:00Z\")); });\n\nit.each([[89, 0], [90, 0], [91, 0.02], [180, 0.02], [181, 0.05]])(\n  \"day %i → %f discount\",\n  (days, rate) => { /* build createdAt relative to the pinned instant */ },\n);\n```\n\nAdd one case whose window spans a DST transition (e.g. `createdAt` in early March, `now` in June under `TZ=America/New_York`) — that's the case the current code gets wrong and that no amount of clock injection alone would catch.\n\n---\n\nOne note on the tool run: `crimes scan src/billing.ts` returned 42 findings across all 21 files (`coverage.files_total: 21`), not just `billing.ts`. Cross-file detectors legitimately need the whole repo, but the path argument didn't scope the reported output, so most of what came back is noise for this question. Might be worth checking whether the path arg is meant to filter the finding set.",
